# The probability of the flip is the variable p, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p.  Where p >= 2 the flip is reached with a probability outside [0, 1]: such a
# run is an error from which no bound may be proved, worth +oo against this cwlp upper bound,
# so the bound fails at every p >= 2.  Elsewhere c = 0 and wlp(S, [c = 1]) = 0 <= 1.
# Expected: unknown.  The reduction asks the lower bound wp(S, 1 - [c = 1]) >= 1 - 1 = 0, and
# an error arm written as the bound minus 1 was the negative constant -1, which the QFL checker
# refuses for the whole input (an error, not an answer).
c := 0;
if p >= 2 {
	if flip(p) {
		c := 1
	}
} else {
	c := 0
}
return c
?Ex[[c = 1]] <= 1 cwlp
